    "result_text": "AcceptanceTest is the only test class, and the full suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 2/2 passing).\n\n## Summary\n\nI redesigned the identity key and duplicate detection to match the spec, touching **3 files** and keeping each changed function small.\n\n**`IdentityKey.java`** \u2014 `of(...)` now returns `SHA-256 hex(normalizedTelephone + '|' + lowerEmail + '|' + soundex(lastName))`. I reused commons-codec's `Soundex.US_ENGLISH` (already on the classpath) rather than writing a soundex implementation, and exposed a small `public static soundex(...)` helper for the soft-match step. The unused `postcode` parameter is retained so `OwnerMapper`'s expression call site (a large interface file) didn't need editing.\n\n**`RequireUniqueIdentity.java`** \u2014 the hard 409 check now compares the full identity key per non-deleted owner instead of just the telephone. Same loop shape, same complexity. The email-domain blocklist still runs first (in the earlier `NormaliseEmail` step) and deleted owners are still skipped.\n\n**`DetectPossibleDuplicate.java`** \u2014 the soft match now triggers when `soundex(lastName)` and postcode match but the identity keys differ (extracted into a tiny `keyOf` helper), also skipping deleted owners. Two owners with the same lastName+postcode but different telephones now get different keys, so they're created as a soft match rather than rejected.\n\nThe former household-duplicate 409 block (`RequireUniqueHousehold`) was already absent from the `owners.POST.yml` pipeline, so no wiring change was needed \u2014 duplicate detection is now the single identity key.",
